6.
How
can
the
2024
COD-PS
update
for
Haiti
be
used?
The
COD-PS
provides
an
updated
set
of
subnational
population
projections,
disaggregated
by
sex
and
five-year
age
groups,
at
ADM-2
geographical
level,
with
a
reference
date
of
September
2024.
It
directly
accounts
for
recent
population
change
due
to
mobility,
mortality
and
fertility.
Across
Haiti,
it
is
used
to:
(1)
Formulate
Humanitarian
Needs
Overview
(HNO)
and
Humanitarian
Response
Plan
(HRP)
across
various
sectors/clusters,
and
(2)
Guide
local-level,
community-led
recovery
programmes.
7.
Is
there
a
difference
in
the
methodology
used
by
the
Haitian
Institute
of
Statistics
and
Informatics
when
forecasting
the
population?
Yes,
there
are
notable
differences
between
official
population
projections
in
Haiti
and
the
2024
COD-PS
update.
These
include:
(1)
The
2024
COD-PS
update
is
produced
by
sex
and
age
for
140
communes
(i.e.
ADM-2
geographic
areas)
across
Haiti,
rather
than
only
at
the
department
(i.e.
ADM-1)
level
as
is
the
case
for
the
official
IHSI
population
projections;
(2)
The
2024
COD-PS
incorporates
additional
data
sources
not
used
in
official
population
projections
by
the
IHSI,
including
recent
operational
data
on
crisis-related
mobility
from
IOM
and
UNHCR
and
previous
Demographic
and
Health
Surveys.;
and
(3)
In
the
past,
official
population
projections
by
IHSI
have
used
a
mathematical
projection
technique
based
on
modeling
growth
rates
of
age-specific
population
totals.
This
is
a
different
technical
approach
used
from
the
2024
COD-PS
update,
which
used
a
cohort-component
projection,
which
focuses
on
estimating
different
components
of
population
change
(mortality,
fertility,
and
mobility)
separately
and
applying
them
to
a
baseline
population.
Given
the
focus
of
the
COD-PS
on
humanitarian
and
recovery
planning
within
the
internationally
recognised
borders
of
Haiti,
the
projected
total
reflected
in
this
COD-PS
excludes
asylum
seekers
and
refugees
from
Haiti
recorded
globally
as
of
December
2023.
8.
How
does
the
2024
COD-PS
update
account
for
recent
mobility
within
and
out
of
Haiti?
The
2024
COD-PS
update
accounts
for
international
migration
and
internal
displacement
within
Haiti,
using
the
best
available
mobility
data.
2024
Haiti
COD-PS
Update
6
End-User
Release
Note
The
2024
COD-PS
update
reflects
both
crisis-related
mobility
as
well
as
other
types
of
mobility
across
international
borders
between
2003
and
2024.
It
accounts
for
international
migration
by
constructing
overall
net
migration
rates
using
the
probabilistic
Bayesian
approach
reflected
in
the
UN
World
Population
Prospects
2024.
The
2024
COD-PS
update
accounts
for
internal
mobility
within
Haiti
using
the
most
recent
IOM
DTM
Mobility
Tracking
Round
8
dataset,
which
measures
the
population
of
internally
displaced
persons
and
returnees
across
Haiti
using
a
key-informant-based
estimation,
and
non-crisis-related
mobility
from
the
2003
census
IPUMS
microdata
sample.
COD-PS
projections
are
closely
aligned
with
insights
from
recent
DTM
data
about
crisis-related
mobility
between
ADM-2
areas
in
Haiti.
9.
What
quality
assurance
and
validation
was
carried
out
on
the
2024
COD-PS
update?
The
2024
COD-PS
update
was
cross-validated
with
comparable
population
estimates
and
projections
at
the
national
level
(ADM-0)
and
at
the
commune
level
(ADM-2).
Cross-validation
sources
included
the
2024
revision
of
the
UN’s
World
Population
Prospects
(WPP,
for
the
ADM-0
geographic
area);
the
International
Database
of
the
United
States
Census
Bureau
(IDB,
for
the
ADM-0
geographic
area);
and
two
sets
of
population
projections
based
on
digital
trace
data,
one
produced
by
Flowminder
Foundation
using
Call
Detail
Records,
and
the
other
produced
from
Impact
Initiatives/REACH
using
social
media
data
from
Meta.
The
cross-validation
analysis
noted
similarities
and
differences
between
these
projections
and
the
2024
COD-PS.
National
population
estimates
were
approximately
consistent
between
the
2024
COD-PS
update,
2024
revision
of
the
UN
WPP,
and
US
Census
International
Database.
The
most
notable
discrepancies
in
subnational
population
estimates
were
observed
in
the
Ouest
department,where
recent
gang-related
violence
is
ongoing,
and
which
has
seen
high
levels
of
crisis-related
mobility,
as
per
recent
IOM
DTM
data.
